          "strip": "p02.s.webp",
          "bytes": 147090,
          "alt": "Opening page 85: On a flat island at the edge of the Atlantic, nine families still make salt by hand, with sun, wind and a wooden rake. Their season lasts eleven weeks, and every year it starts a little later."
        },
        {
          "n": 3,
          "label": "86",
          "role": "body",
          "w": 1000,
          "h": 1320,
          "file": "p03.webp",
          "strip": "p03.s.webp",
          "bytes": 228710,
          "alt": "Page 86: Water, sun and wind. Raking the crystallisers at dawn. The salt rolls up in a ridge; a rake that digs in tears the clay floor and greys the harvest."
        },
        {
          "n": 4,
          "label": "87",
          "role": "body",
          "w": 1000,
          "h": 1320,
          "file": "p04.webp",
          "strip": "p04.s.webp",
          "bytes": 176898,
          "alt": "Page 87: The flower of the salt. The Alvarra flats from the lighthouse hill. The pink basins are nearly ready: an alga colours the brine once it holds more than 200 grams of salt a litre."
        },
        {
          "n": 5,
          "label": "88",
          "role": "body",
          "w": 1000,
          "h": 1320,
          "file": "p05.webp",
          "strip": "p05.s.webp",
          "bytes": 222862,
          "alt": "Page 88: The herons’ share. Skimming the flower: the sieve works the downwind edge of the pan, where the breeze has drifted the crust. · Amélia Sousa with an afternoon’s flor de sal, about four kilos from two pans."
        },
        {
          "n": 6,
          "label": "89",
          "role": "body",
          "w": 1000,
          "h": 1320,
          "file": "p06.webp",
          "strip": "p06.s.webp",
          "bytes": 152004,
          "alt": "Page 89. The Alvarra pans (pink) lie on the eastern flats, 6 km from Porto Velho (the squares) by the coast road. The lighthouse hill is the triangle; the ferry, dashed. · Porto Velho, the last week of September: fifty-kilo sacks wait for the mainland boat."
        }
